Harston had no need for gods as long as he had a dagger by his side.
He was confident that he could retrieve this bauble without invoking
so-called divine help.  If it existed.  Anyway, what had the gods ever
done for him?

He'd heard rumours in the pub about two places in the shallow regions
of the Mazes that sounded like they were worth visiting - a cave system
called Sokoban and a Gnomish town in some mines.  There was meant to be
a treasure at the top of Sokoban, so he was planning to head there
first.

After an uneventful few levels (he really couldn't see what all the
fuss was about) Harston came upon Delphi, and the Oracle standing
amongst the fountains.  Harston had no time for the mindless ramblings
of that half-wit, so he continued on his way to Sokoban.  He quickly
found a bag at the top which seemed to make everything lighter.  This
could prove very useful for keeping all of his treasure in!  And what
better place to find treasure than Minetown, with all of it's shops?

Disappointingly, there was nothing very interesting in the town, but
when he held a dagger to a gnome's throat he learned about greater
treasures deeper in the mines.  Wiping the blood off his hands, he
decided to head down and have a look around.  On the way to the bottom
he came across a potion of water that seemed strangely different to the
others in his pack.  He dipped some other potions of water into it and
they glowed with a light blue aura.  This must be holy water - he
decided to try dipping other things into it as well, but he'd have to
wait until he could find a water source.

Finally he found a level where there weren't any more stairs going
down.  While exploring he found a mysterious lamp that never seemed to
run out of power.  He blessed it with the holy water, and rubbed it.  A
djinni emerged and granted him a wish!  After a lot of thought, he
wished for a blessed fixed +3 cloak of magic resistance, the most
powerful item he'd ever heard of.  An ornamental cope appeared, and he
hurriedly removed his elven cloak and put it on.  Although he didn't
entirely trust the djinni, the way the cloak shimmered in the light
made him sure that it did indeed have magical properties.

Just around the corner he ran into a blue dragon!  It was a hard fight
but eventually he beat it.  When he ate the corpse his health felt
amplified.  Just before heading back to Minetown he read a very cheap
scroll he'd picked up in Minetown which he'd worked out was blessed.
Suddenly he knew all sorts of things about the junk he was carrying!
This helped him when sorting through all of his possessions.  By this
point two of his three pets had died, but his pet giant ant had stepped
on a polytrap and become a black naga.  He went back to the polytrap
and turned it into a baby black dragon, and trained it on the way back
to Minetown until it became an adult.  Now it could eat all the
shopkeepers!

He cleared out Minetown, although he took pity on the kindly old man in
the lighting store and locked him safely indoors away from the dragon.
He then went back into the main dungeon and decided to start looking
for the Amulet in earnest.  He descended to dlvl 8, where he came upon
a huge general store.  After having a careful look around, he let his
dragon in to eat the shopkeeper, and then emptied it of all useful
items.  One of the pairs of boots was a +2 pair of speed boots!  It
took a while to sort through all the stuff, but he was lucky enough to
get another full inventory ID from a blessed ID scroll.

One level down on dlvl9 he very nearly died inside an air elemental.
He was on 16 HP inside the elemental with no means of escape.  Taking a
deep breath, he took 2 potions of extra healing out of his bag of
holding.  Now on 5 HP, he quaffed both of them to get up to 30 HP and
then threw 7 daggers into the elemental.  He managed to kill it with 20
HP remaining.  He found a poly trap on that level - perhaps that was
where the elemental had come from.

On dlvl 11 he received a telepathic message from the Master of Thieves,
but when he tried to go to his aid he was told that we wasn't
experienced enough.  He'd have to come back later.

Level 17 seemed like a very primitive area of the dungeon, with very
roughly hewn stone walls with strange (almost childish) symbols
scrawled on them.  He descended quickly, not wanting to hang around too
long.  He figured that the bottom of the dungeon must be nearby.

Little of interest happened during this descent.  He found another
magic lamp on dlvl 18.  Also on dlvl 18 he came across an unfriendly
elf-lord as he was digging out a vault.  On killing him he discovered a
pair of gloves made him feel very dextrous.  On putting them on he
realised that they had been enchanted 5 times!  A holy man on dlvl 19
became dragon food.  On dlvl 20 he paused and used the marker he'd
found to write some enchant armour scrolls and enchanted his boots and
his cloak of magic resistance to +5.

On dlvl 22 he read a scroll of identify and discovered that the cursed
helm he'd found a couple of levels above was a helm of opposite
alignment.  He stuck it in his bag of holding in case he found a use
for it later.  Then he ran into a couple of long worms which left teeth
behind.  He enchanted one of them and it became a crysknife, and he
started using that as his primary weapon.

On dlvl 24 he ran into a throne room and found four dragons - gray,
black, orange and yellow.  He killed them in partnership with his pet
black dragon and ate the corpse of the black one, which made him feel
very firm.

On dlvl 26 he came upon a mysterious swamp which he had no means of
crossing.  He decided to drink his potions of gain level and gain
enough experience to assist the Master of Thieves and then headed back
up the dungeon.  He stashed some of his gear outside the portal to
Ransmannsby, and then went inside.  Finding lots of chameleons, he
locked his dragon in a room and went to explore.  He learned about the
Master Assassin, and vowed to go and recover the Master Key of Thievery
from the caverns.

Reaching the bottom of the caverns, he realised that he would need to
try jumping through holes in the penultimate level in order to land in
the right chamber.  He went back to his stash in the main dungeon and
used a scroll of charging to recharge his magic marker, wrote a few
cursed scrolls of teleport, donned his ring of teleport control and
went to see the Master Assassin.  Having completed his mission, he went
back to Ransmannsby, and was told by the Master of Thieves to keep the
key that he'd found!

Back through the magic portal to his stash, and he identified the
gloves he'd found on the quest as a -5 pair of gauntlets of power.
He'd have to wait until he could enchant those.

He then decided to descend back to the swamp to see if he could work
out how to cross it.  On dlvl 15, he found a vault he'd not previously
seen which contained another magic portal.  As soon as he stepped
through it, an alarm sounded!  Cursing, he went back to the dungeon to
find his dragon.  Looked like he'd have to do this job the hard way...

It was a tough fight through the first couple of rooms, and he met four
dragons in the gardens, but eventually he got round to the front of a
huge castle.  Now he just needed a way in...  He bridged the moat with
ice using a wand of cold and then made short work of Croesus.  There
was a huge treasure vault hidden behind the throne room.  While he was
liberating the gold a captain killed his pet dragon.  He killed the
captain, but getting a new pet was now an urgent goal.  He kept his
pet's scales in the hope of one day turning them into armour.

Back at the stash he identified the amulets he'd picked up in Fort
Ludios and discovered that one of them was reflection - very handy!
Heading back up to the polymorph trap he'd found on level 9, he tamed a
couple of cats and very quickly had a silver dragon and a baby gray
dragon as pets.  Now it was time to find that swamp again.  He got down
there easily enough, and decided to bridge the water with his wands of
cold (he had two in his pack).  It worked surprisingly well.  He had
been warned about Medusa, and wrapped a towel carefully around his head
before slipping into her room and throwing daggers at her.  She didn't
last long!  A statue of Perseus next to her was holding a very shiny
shield, so he took that to replace his dwarven roundshield.

Below the swamp was a maze, and below that what appeared to be the
entrance to an underground castle.  He'd never suspected that this
mythical castle actually existed - but here he was at the entrance!  He
bridged the moat with a wand of cold and then destroyed the drawbridge
with a wand of striking.  The ensuing fight was relatively easy,
although annoyingly some of his daggers were disenchanted and
un-rustproofed by disenchanters and rust monsters respectively.  He had
some more in his stash, but he was going to have to be very careful
with those species...

After the fight he discovered his way forwards blocked by a trap door
in a corridor.  He zapped the wand of wishing which he'd found in a
nearby chest and asked for a ring of levitation.  Now he could kill
those dragons he'd seen!  He killed them easily (although his pet gray
and silver dragons died in the assault).  He then fell into a hole and
found himself in the middle of a horde of undead.  He switched to his
silver sabre, which welded itself to his hand, but he had bigger things
to worry about right now.  After a long fight he had cleared the level
and decided to go back to his stash to take stock.

After a good long think he decided to use his wand of wishing to get
some useful things.  He asked for a helm of brilliance, two magic
markers, a book of identify and a t-shirt.  He also enchanted his
former pet's scales to make Black Dragon Scale Mail.  It took a long
time to practice his spellcasting, but eventually he knew identify well
enough to only fail 22% of the time.  He then set about identifying all
of his possessions.  Once this was complete he enchanted his stack of
daggers, chrysknife and silver sabre to +7.  He #twoweaponed the sabre
and the chrisknife, and kept the daggers in his quiver for later.  He
experimented with mixing potions and managed to make 19 blessed potions
of full healing, which made him feel very healthy when he quaffed them.
 Hitching up his dragon scale mail, he headed down the stairs to find
out what lay beneath the Valley of the Dead.

Harston had never suspect that the very entrance to Hell stood
underneath the Mazes of Menace.  Despite the heat, he decided to press
on.  He figured that he could always head back if it got too difficult,
and thoughts of the treasures that may lie in wait compelled him to
proceed.

After killing Asmodeus, Jubilex and Baalzebub he headed back to the
stash.  On the way back down he accidentally threw a dagger at the
priestess of Moloch in the Valley of the Dead.  She didn't seem to be
in a mood to forgive him so he killed her.  Molock was not best
pleased.

Continuing his exploration of Hell, he came upon a ghostly town.  A
demon called Orcus was guarding it - he threw a potion of paralysis and
Orcus' head and then killed him easily.  He was rewarded with a wand of
death.

Below Orcus' town Harston found some stairs up into a little tower.  At
the top he found and killed Vlad the Impaler and recovered a very nice
little trinket - a seven-stemmed candelabrum.  That would be worth a
few bob back at the pub.  He also found a ring of increase damage and a
scroll of genocide.

Harston eventually got down a such a deep level that he couldn't even
dig in the floor.  There was a mysterious vibrating square which he
carefully marked the position of, and then decided to head back up to
his stash to sort everything out.  He had a feeling that something
important lay beyond that square, and he wanted to be ready.  On the
way up he passed a gray dragon in the castle which left scales.  He
grabbed a slippery cloak out of the armour room there and enchanted
both to +5.

He sorted through his gear, and stuffed all the treasure into a spare
bag of holding (by this point he had 3).  Scrawling SWAG on the side,
he dumped it by the exit to the dungeon.  They were never going to
believe this in the pub!

It didn't take long to figure out how to get past the vibrating square.
 Entering the cavern below, he discovered that all the zombies and
wraiths seemed to be asleep, and he made short work of them.  The
priests were a little tougher but not too bad.  Suddenly a Balrog
whipped his chrysknife out of his hands.  He killed it, but the
chrysknife fell to the floor and immediately turned back into a worm
tooth.  He had no scrolls of enchant weapon left, so decided to use his
spare magic lamp to wish for some.  Unfortunately, the idiot djinni
merely thanked him and did not grant him a wish.  After some
consideration he decided to use his only remaining magic lamp (his
light source) to try and wish for the scrolls.  With two oil lamps he
was confident that he could get out of the dungeon without running out
of light.  He took a deep breath, rubbed the lamp, and nothing
happened.  A few rubs later, though, the djinni popped out and granted
him the wish he'd wanted.

After this interlude, Harston carried on hacking his way to the high
altar, and polished off the priest without too much difficulty.
Finally, the Amulet of Yendor was his!  Now all he had to do was get to
the surface!

He started up through the mazes of Hell.  A mysterious force pulled him
back a few times, and then suddenly the Wizard of Yendor appeared!
After a brief fight he was dead once more, but he reappeared several
times on the way back up.  At long last Harston wearily stepped back
onto the shallowest level, the same level where the whole adventure had
started.  It seemed like weeks ago, but it was probably only a few
days.  He grabbed his SWAG bag and then made for the exit - only to be
transported to the Plane of Earth.

He hadn't been counting on this.  The Wizard was there again, and had
to be taken care of.  He dug around and eventually found another
portal, but this one took him to the Plane of Air.  He had quite a lot
of difficulty finding the exit, but eventually stepped into another
portal to the Plane of Fire.  Suddenly he remembered that he had a
scroll of gold detection and a few potions of booze, so he confused
himself and then read the scroll to reveal the location of the portal.
Diving in, he found himself on the Plane of Water.  It took a long,
long time to find the exit, and he had to kill the Wizard another 4
times.  Eventually he found it and was teleported to the Astral Plane.

Immediately he was surrounded by 2 Master Liches who summoned in lots
of monsters.  He engraved Elbereth and then fought them off.  Heading
left, he ran into Famine and zapped him with a wand of death.  He made
his way through the hordes of priests, priestesses and angels and
finally made it to the altar.  Luckily it turned out to be the right
one.  Despite his disgust at the thought, he knew that the only way out
was to sacrifice the Amulet of Yendor - the first pious act of his
life.  Taking a deep breath, he stepped up to the altar...

